[5] Fundamental readout
  1. LRC14 is no longer looking like one master scalar.  It is a
     layered obstruction calculus: owner-essential boundary H1 first,
     status-preserving coarse gates second, route teeth third, and
     residual capacitors/zeta squares only at the end.
  2. The AP/GW equality atom is topological and deletion-critical,
     not merely a tight maximin value.
  3. The sharp q-boundary is q<=13 versus q=14: q<=13 primitive safe
     mass is direct-witness currency; q=14 is boundary/covering currency.
  4. Owner identity is repeatedly the missing coordinate: first teeth are
     owner strips, endpoint-current words can be lossy, and deleting any
     AP/GW owner kills the closed-H1 representative.
  5. Past scalar shadows become useful once labelled by their blindness:
     automatic words are row/column shadows, mu^2/phi is a squarefree
     capacity meter, and pair-good decoys are blocker-generator teeth.

[6] Sidecar theorem target
  Add a hidden-statement sidecar vocabulary to the HYP-2963 packet ledger:
    boundary_h1_owner_support, first_tooth, primitive_safe_deck_2_13,
    residual_capacitor_id, first_cut_stage, exact_M_zeta,
    endpoint_owner_strip, analytic_blindness_report, automaton_shadow_class.
  Candidate principle:
    Every primitive packet either carries the AP/GW owner-essential
    boundary H1 atom, has a q<=13 primitive witness, descends through
    an owner/stalk/topology tooth, or exposes a named capacitor/zeta
    cut leading to covering, petal, K33/THM-572, Haar/Fejer/Ramanujan
    annihilation, or F7 debt.
